{%- comment -%}
  AJ Email Popup — the capture that interrupts.

  A SNIPPET, NOT A SECTION, AND THAT IS THE FIX. As a static section in the
  layout this rendered nothing at all, three ways: a section never opened in the
  theme editor has no stored settings, Shopify does not fall back to schema
  defaults for those, and Liquid evaluates `nil == false` as TRUE — so `if
  enabled`, `if enabled != false`, and everything in between produced an empty
  wrapper. A snippet has no settings layer to be nil, so it simply renders.

  The cost is that the copy lives in this file rather than the theme editor.
  Worth it: the copy changes rarely, and a popup nobody can see is worth nothing
  however configurable it is.

  IT EARNS ITS INTERRUPTION OR IT DOES NOT APPEAR — the suppression rules live in
  aj-email-popup.js and matter more than anything here:
    never on the join page, never after a signup, never inside the dismissal
    window, never before the delay, and never at all without localStorage to
    keep those promises with.

  THE OFFER IS 10% OFF FULL-PRICE ITEMS, AND THE OLD NOTE HERE WAS NOT WRONG.
  It read "the offer is access, not a discount — the Yoink is clamped near cost
  and the bin runs at a 20% floor, so another discount comes out of a $12.67
  contribution." That is exactly true of a discount that can reach the Yoink and
  the bin, and it is why this one cannot: the code runs through
  `extensions/signup-discount`, which excludes every product tagged
  `yoink-of-the-day`, `bargain-bin` or `tee-of-the-week`. Measured on the live
  catalogue 6 Aug: of 2,793 full-priced products, 2,403 keep 15%+ margin at 10%
  off and 12 fall below cost, most of those from supplier costs already known to
  be corrupt.

  THE CONSTRAINT IS ON THE PANEL, NOT ONLY IN THE EMAIL. "Full-price items only"
  and "one use per person" are stated where somebody decides whether to hand
  over an address, because finding out at checkout that the code does not apply
  to the thing you came for is how a signup becomes a complaint. The 10% is
  still the headline; the limit is the line under it.

  AND IT PROMISES NO CADENCE. This said "one email a day", which committed the
  business to a send frequency nobody had decided on — and an unkept promise made
  at the moment someone hands over their address is the most expensive kind.
  "When something's worth your time" is true whatever the schedule turns out to
  be, and it can tighten later once a real cadence exists. Never loosen a promise
  after making it; only ever make one you already keep.

  NEVER PUT A LIQUID COMMENT BETWEEN THE ATTRIBUTES BELOW. `{%- ... -%}` strips
  the whitespace on BOTH sides of itself, so a comment sitting between `hidden`
  and `data-delay` does not leave a gap where it was — it welds them into one
  junk attribute, `hiddendata-delay="10"`. The element then has no `hidden`, so
  the popup renders instantly on page load instead of after the delay, and no
  `data-delay` either. It shipped exactly once, on 6 Aug, and the symptom was
  "comes up immediately and won't close". Notes about these attributes go up
  here, where they cannot touch the markup.

  DATA-DELAY IS 10 SECONDS. The floor on that number is the Yoink: the deal
  should be the first thing a visitor sees, and reading a product, a price, a
  saving and a countdown takes a few seconds. Much under ten and the popup lands
  on somebody mid-sentence, which is how a capture teaches people to close
  things without reading them.

  It was briefly cut to seven on 8 Aug, on the argument that 95% of paid traffic
  leaves before viewing a product so the timer was firing into an empty room.
  True, and not the whole picture: a popup arriving before anyone has read
  anything is not capture, it is an interruption. Restored to Alex's ten.

  DATA-SCROLL-AFTER IS 10 TOO, so ten seconds is a real floor rather than a
  claim about one trigger. It gates BOTH the mobile scroll trigger and desktop
  exit intent — set it lower than the delay and the popup appears sooner than
  ten to anyone who scrolls or moves their mouse, which makes restoring the
  timer a setting change with no behaviour change. Drop it below the delay only
  if engaged readers should deliberately be asked sooner than everyone else.

  DATA-SCROLL-DEPTH IS 30 PERCENT of the page, and it is the mobile half of the
  same rule: a phone has no cursor, so scroll depth is the only available
  evidence that somebody is reading rather than bouncing. Both conditions have
  to hold.

  AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS NOT A HISTORICAL NOTE. On 8 Aug I wrote exactly
  these explanations as Liquid comment blocks between the attributes, three
  paragraphs below the warning telling me not to, and shipped
  `hiddendata-delay="7"data-scroll-after="5"` to live traffic. Same symptom as
  6 Aug, same cause, same file. The attribute list stays bare.

  Nor can the comment TAG be written out literally anywhere in this block, not
  even inside backticks: Liquid does not nest them, so the first endcomment
  closes the outer one and the rest of the file becomes markup inside a comment
  that never ends. That is the second thing I broke writing this note.
{%- endcomment -%}
<aj-email-popup
    class="aj-pop"
    hidden
    data-delay="10"
    data-scroll-after="10"
    data-scroll-depth="30"
    data-remember-days="30"
    data-exit-intent="true"
  >
    <div class="aj-pop__backdrop" data-close></div>

    <div class="aj-pop__panel" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="aj-pop-title">
      <button class="aj-pop__x" type="button" data-close aria-label="Close">&times;</button>

      <div data-state="form">
        <p class="aj-pop__eyebrow" aria-hidden="true">New here?</p>
        <h2 class="aj-pop__title" id="aj-pop-title">Take <span class="aj-pop__pct">10%</span> off</h2>

          <p class="aj-pop__sub">Your own one-time code, on anything at full price. Plus a new deal every morning and a Bargain Bin that rotates weekly.</p>


        {%- form 'customer', class: 'aj-pop__form' -%}
          <input type="hidden" name="contact[tags]" value="popup">
          <label class="visually-hidden" for="aj-pop-email">Email address</label>
          <input
            id="aj-pop-email"
            class="aj-pop__input"
            type="email"
            name="contact[email]"
            placeholder="you@example.com"
            autocomplete="email"
            required
          >
          <button class="aj-pop__submit" type="submit">Count me in</button>
          
            <p class="aj-pop__fine">Full-price items only &mdash; the Yoink of the Day, the Bargain Bin and the Tee of the Week are already discounted. One use per person. No spam, unsubscribe anytime.</p>
          
        {%- endform -%}

        <button class="aj-pop__decline" type="button" data-close>
          No thanks
        </button>
      </div>

      {%- comment -%}
        THE SUCCESS STATE, which did not exist until 7 Aug 2026.

        `data-state="form"` above implied a second state from the day it shipped
        and there was never one. Shopify posts the form and reloads the page, the
        script recorded the signup and returned, and the popup simply VANISHED —
        so the only confirmation was the theme's own message rendering down in
        the footer, which is also what the `#contact_form` anchor scrolls you to.
        Somebody handed over an address and got a page jump for it.

        ONLY SHOWN FOR THE POPUP'S OWN FORM. Every signup on the store comes back
        to `?customer_posted=true`, including the deal panel's — and that one
        receives tomorrow's preview, NOT a discount code. Promising 10% here
        would be a lie on that path, so the script sets a marker on submit and
        only this form's submissions land on this copy.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div data-state="success" hidden>
        <p class="aj-pop__eyebrow" aria-hidden="true">You're in</p>
        <h2 class="aj-pop__title" id="aj-pop-done">Check your <span class="aj-pop__pct">inbox</span></h2>
        <p class="aj-pop__sub">
          Your code is on its way &mdash; yours alone, good for one order on anything at full price.
        </p>
        <button class="aj-pop__submit" type="button" data-close>Start shopping</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </aj-email-popup>

  <script src="{{ 'aj-email-popup.js' | asset_url }}" type="module" fetchpriority="low"></script>

<style>
  .aj-pop { position: fixed; inset: 0; z-index: 1000; display: block; }
  .aj-pop[hidden] { display: none; }

  .aj-pop__backdrop {
    position: absolute; inset: 0;
    background: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.5);
    animation: aj-pop-fade 0.25s ease;
  }

  /*
    BRAND PURPLE, NOT THEME WHITE. Every other surface on this store is one of
    the four brand colours — teal header, yellow story panel, purple footer —
    and a plain white sheet was the only thing on the page that could have come
    from any Shopify theme. The yellow top edge is the same 2px idiom the cart
    upsell already uses.
  */
  /*
    CENTRED, BUT NEVER TALLER THAN THE SCREEN. With `top: 50%` and a -50%
    translate, a panel taller than the viewport overflows in BOTH directions —
    and the half that goes off the top takes the close button with it. On a
    short phone in landscape, or with large text, the × was simply unreachable
    and the only way out was the back button.

    `max-height` plus `overflow-y` keeps the panel inside the screen and lets
    its own content scroll instead. `100dvh` because mobile browser chrome makes
    `100vh` taller than the space actually visible, which is the exact error
    this is correcting; `100vh` stays as the fallback for anything that does not
    know `dvh`.
  */
  .aj-pop__panel {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%; top: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    width: min(30rem, calc(100vw - 2rem));
    max-height: calc(100vh - 2rem);
    max-height: calc(100dvh - 2rem);
    overflow-y: auto;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    padding: 2.25rem 2rem 2rem;
    border-radius: 14px;
    border-top: 4px solid var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
    background: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
    color: #fff;
    box-shadow: 0 24px 60px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.42);
    animation: aj-pop-rise 0.28s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.3, 1);
  }

  @keyframes aj-pop-fade { from { opacity: 0 } to { opacity: 1 } }
  @keyframes aj-pop-rise {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translate(-50%, calc(-50% + 12px)); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .aj-pop__backdrop, .aj-pop__panel { animation: none; }
  }

  /*
    A REAL TAP TARGET. This was a bare glyph with no padding — about 18x28px of
    hittable area, against the 44x44 minimum a thumb needs, at 0.5 opacity on a
    dark purple panel. Small, faint, and the primary way out of a dialog.

    ABSOLUTE, NOT STICKY-AND-FLOATED. The first attempt at enlarging this used
    `position: sticky` with `float: right` and negative margins, reasoning that
    a scrolling panel would carry an absolute button out of view. Float and
    sticky together place unpredictably — the button landed over the title and
    was harder to hit than the tiny one it replaced. The panel is capped to the
    viewport and its content is short, so the scroll case it was solving barely
    exists; pinning to the panel's own top-right corner is what a person
    expects and what reliably works.

    `z-index` because it now overlaps the title's box, and a 44px target that
    sits UNDER the heading is a 44px target that does nothing.
  */
  .aj-pop__x {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.35rem;
    right: 0.35rem;
    z-index: 2;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 44px; height: 44px;
    background: none; border: 0; cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 1.75rem; line-height: 1; opacity: 0.75;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .aj-pop__x:hover, .aj-pop__x:focus-visible { opacity: 1; }

  /*
    SCALE IS WHAT MAKES THE DISPLAY FACE WORK. `.aj-pop__title` was already
    getting Flashy from aj-brand.css, but at 1.5rem — which is precisely the
    size that file records as rendering "thin and cramped" when the deal price
    was tried in it. The face was there and starved. Same fix as the price:
    give it room rather than reaching for more decoration.
  */
  .aj-pop__title {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 7vw, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }

  /* The number is the offer, so it is the one thing in brand yellow. */
  .aj-pop__pct { color: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000); }

  .aj-pop__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .aj-pop__sub { margin: 0 0 1.25rem; opacity: 0.85; line-height: 1.45; }

  .aj-pop__form { display: grid; gap: 0.7rem; }

  .aj-pop__input {
    box-sizing: border-box; width: 100%;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem; font-size: 1rem;
    border-radius: 8px;
    border: 2px solid transparent;
    background: #fff;
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
  }
  .aj-pop__input:focus {
    outline: none;
    border-color: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
  }

  /*
    YELLOW ON PURPLE IS THE LOUDEST PAIR THE BRAND OWNS, which is what the one
    action in here should be. Deliberately NOT in the display face: at button
    size Flashy cramps, exactly as it did on the price — weight in the text face
    carries a call to action better than personality does.
  */
  .aj-pop__submit {
    padding: 0.95rem 1rem; font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 800;
    border: 0; border-radius: 8px; cursor: pointer;
    background: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
  }
  .aj-pop__submit:hover { filter: brightness(1.08); }

  .aj-pop__fine { margin: 0; font-size: 0.78rem; opacity: 0.7; line-height: 1.4; }

  /* A plain, low-contrast decline rather than a guilt-tripped "no thanks, I hate
     savings". Dark patterns buy one signup and cost the brand. */
  .aj-pop__decline {
    margin-top: 0.9rem; width: 100%;
    background: none; border: 0; cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.55; color: inherit;
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .aj-pop__decline:hover { opacity: 0.85; }
</style>
